2 CONT
2 CONT =Antiochus IV of Commagene=
2 CONT '''Gaius Julius Antiochus IV Epiphanes''' (Greek: Γάιος Ἰούλιος Ἀντίοχος ὀ Ἐπιφανής, before 17 CE – after 72 CE), the last king of Commagene, reigned between 38-72 as a client king to the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Life==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Antiochus''' was a prince and son of '''Antiochus III of Commagene''' and his mother was '''Queen Iotapa of Commagene'''. 
2 CONT *''The parents of Antiochus IV were full-blooded siblings, who had married each other.'' 
2 CONT *''His own sister and later wife was '''Iotapa'''''. 
2 CONT *He was of Armenian,[1] Greek and Medes descent. Through his ancestor from Commagene, '''Queen Laodice VII Thea''', who was the mother of '''King Antiochus I Theos of Commagene''', he was a direct descendant of the Greek Syrian Ki
2 CONC ngdom the Seleucid Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT
2 CONT In 43 his first son, '''Gaius Julius Archelaus Antiochus Epiphanes''', was betrothed to '''Drusilla''', a daughter of '''Agrippa I'''.[6]  Apart from Epiphanes, Antiochus had another two children with Iotapa: '''Callinicus''' and 
2 CONC a '''younger Iotapa'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antiochus_IV_of_Commagene
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaius Julius Antiochus IV Epiphanes (Greek: Γάιος Ἰούλιος Ἀντίοχος ὀ Ἐπιφανής, before 17 CE – after 72 CE) was the last king of Commagene, who reigned between 38-72 as a client king to the Roman Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [show] 1 Life2 Coinage3 See also4 Notes5 References6 External links  [edit] Life Antiochus was a prince and son of Antiochus III of Commagene and his mother was Queen Iotapa of Commagene. The parents of Antiochus IV were 
2 CONC full-blooded siblings, who had married each other. His own sister and later wife was Iotapa. He was of Armenian,[1] Greek and Medes descent. Through his ancestor from Commagene, Queen Laodice VII Thea, who was the mother of King A
2 CONC ntiochus I Theos of Commagene, he was a direct descendant of the Greek Syrian Kingdom the Seleucid Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus appears to have been very young when in 17, his father died. Roman Emperor Tiberius agreed with the citizens of Commagene to make their kingdom a part of the Roman province of Syria. Between 17 and 38, Antiochus seems to
2 CONC  have gained Roman citizenship. He lived and was raised in Rome, along with his sister. While he and his sister were growing up in Rome, they were part of the remarkable court of Antonia Minor, a niece of the first Roman Emperor A
2 CONC ugustus and the youngest daughter of triumvir Mark Antony. Antonia Minor was a very influential woman and supervised her circle of various princes and princesses. Her circle assisted in the political preservation of the Roman Empi
2 CONC re’s borders and affairs of the client states.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 38, Antiochus received his paternal dominion from Antonia’s grandson, the Roman Emperor Caligula. In addition, the emperor enlarged Antiochus' territory with a part of Cilicia bordering on the seacoast. Caligula also gave him t
2 CONC he whole amount of the revenues of Commagene during the twenty years that it had been a Roman province.[2][3] The reasons for providing a client king with such vast resources remain unclear; it was perhaps a stroke of Caligula's w
2 CONC ell-attested eccentricity. Antiochus was on most intimate terms with Caligula, and he and King Agrippa I are spoken of as the instructors of the emperor in the art of tyranny.[4] This friendship, however, did not last very long, f
2 CONC or he was subsequently deposed by Caligula.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus did not obtain his kingdom again till the accession of Roman Emperor Claudius in 41.[5] In 43 his first son, Gaius Julius Archelaus Antiochus Epiphanes, was betrothed to Drusilla, a daughter of Agrippa I.[6] Apart from E
2 CONC piphanes, Antiochus had another two children with Iotapa: Callinicus and a younger Iotapa. In 53, Antiochus put down an insurrection of some barbarous tribes in Cilicia, called Clitae.[7] In 55, he received orders from Roman Emper
2 CONC or Nero to levy troops to make war against the Parthians, and in the year 59, he served under General Gnaeus Domitius Corbulo against King Tiridates I of Armenia, brother of the Parthian King Vologases I of Parthia.[8] In conseque
2 CONC nce of his services in this war, in the year 61 he obtained parts of Armenia.[9]
2 CONT
2 CONT He took the side of Roman Emperor Vespasian when the latter was proclaimed emperor in 70; and he is then spoken of as the richest of the tributary kings.[10] In the same year he sent forces, commanded by his son Epiphanes, to assi
2 CONC st prince Titus in the siege of Jerusalem.[11][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT Antiochus' downfall came only two years afterwards, in 72, when he was accused by Lucius Caesennius Paetus, the governor of Syria, of conspiring with the Parthians against the Romans. He was therefore deprived of his kingdom, afte
2 CONC r a reign of thirty-four years from his first appointment by Caligula. Antiochus' sons, princes Epiphanes and Callinicus, fled to Parthia after a brief encounter with Roman troops. Antiochus himself first retired to Sparta, and th
2 CONC en to Rome, where he passed the remainder of his life with his sons Epiphanes and Callinicus and was treated with great respect.[13] Among the grandchildren of Antiochus and Iotapa was the prominent Athenian citizen Philopappos wh
2 CONC o lived in Greece between the 1st century and 2nd century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coinage There are several coins of this king extant, and their die-marks prove he did rule large parts of Cappadocia and Cilicia as well as Commagene proper. In one of those coins he is called ΒΑΣΙΛΕΥΣ ΜΕΓΑΣ ΑΝΤΙΟΧΟΣ ("Great King 
2 CONC Antiochus"), a testament to his political ambitions. On the reverse of that coin a scorpion is represented, surrounded with the foliage of the laurel, and inscribed ΚΟΜΜΑΓΗΝΩΝ ("of the Commagenians"). From his coins we also learn 
2 CONC the name of his wife, Iotapa.[14][15]
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
