1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Adarnase III (Georgian: ადარნასე III), of the Nersianid Dynasty, was a presiding prince of Iberia (Kartli, eastern Georgia) from c. 748 to 760.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase was a son of Prince Nerse I Nersiani by his wife, third daughter of Mirian of Kakheti.
2 CONT
2 CONT Originally a hereditary duke (eristavi) of Inner Iberia, he seems to have succeeded the Guaramid ruler Guaram III whose son was married to Adarnase's daughter. He was succeeded by his son, Nerse.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adarnase's title of curopalates testifies to the degree of Byzantine influence in Georgia even though the Caliphate suzerainty continued.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nersianids (Nersiani; Georgian: ნერსიანი) were an early medieval Georgian princely family. Appearing in the late 5th century, in the reign of King Vakhtang I of Iberia (c. 447-522), they figure in the eighth as dukes of Inner 
2 CONC Iberia and twice attained, in the persons of Adarnase III and his son Nerse, to the office of Presiding Prince (erismtavari) of Iberia between c. 748 and 779/80. The first occupant of this office, Adarnase, held, in addition, the 
2 CONC high Byzantine title of curopalates.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The family was related to another leading Georgian princely family, the Guaramids, through the marriage of the Guaramid Guaram III’s son to a daughter of Adarnase III. The second Nersianid ruling prince Nerse was dispossessed by t
2 CONC he Arabs and his office was given to his nephew (sister’s son) Stephanus II.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adarnase_III_of_Iberia
