1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_I_of_Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James I of Cyprus''' (or '''Jacques I de Lusignan''') (1334 – September 9, 1398) was Regent of Cyprus for his infant nephew Peter from 1369. When Peter died in 1382, James became King of Cyprus that year. James was also titular
2 CONC  King of Armenia and titular King of Jerusalem 1382–1398.
2 CONT
2 CONT James was the third son of Hugh IV of Cyprus and Alix of Ibelin, and became king upon the death of his nephew Peter II. Before becoming a king, he had other offices and was known for his resistance against the Genoese invasion aga
2 CONC inst Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Noble and regent=
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of his father Hugh IV, James' half-brother Guy, titular Prince of Galilee was already dead and his eldest brother Peter I, who reigned for 10 years, was then murdered. The latter's son, Peter II, who was a minor, b
2 CONC egan his reign when he came of age. Meanwhile, Peter I's wife Eleanor of Aragon to revenge her husband's death, invited the Genoese to invade Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the Genoese had commercial and financial interests in Cyprus, they invaded the island in April 1373. After achieving the takeover of the well-fortified city of Famagusta, they arrested and held captive Peter II and his mothe
2 CONC r Eleanor who had invited them. After they killed the nobles who had murdered Peter I, they wanted to take control of the island. After the end of the war, Eleanor succeeded the murder of John, which she claimed she was responsibl
2 CONC e for her husband's murder.
2 CONT
2 CONT James married his kinswoman Helvis of Brunswick-Grubenhagen (1353 – January 15/25, 1421) (daughter of Philip of Brunswick, Constable of Jerusalem and Helisia of Dampierre) in 1365. Her brother John of Brunswick-Grubenhagen (d. Jun
2 CONC e 11, 1414 unmarried and without issue) was an Admiral of Cyprus and their father Philip of Brunswick-Grubenhagen (ca. 1332 – August 4, 1369/1370) was a Constable of the Kingdom of Jerusalem.
2 CONT
2 CONT James was created Constable of Cyprus, and in that office, led the war against the Genoese in 1372.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the invasion, the other two sons of Guy, James and John, resisted the invasion. James fought well in Kyrenia, resisting the Genoese attack, a resistance that was victorious at the end, under the command of James. However, h
2 CONC is nephew Peter II, signed a Treaty with Genoese, who kept Famagusta and in the Treaty, James had to leave from Cyprus. James, stopped the war and left the island with a ship from Kyrenia in 1374 and went to Europe. At first he we
2 CONC nt to Rhodes, where he found no help and he was arrested by Genoese and went in Genoa as a captive with his wife. With the capture of Kyrenia in 1374, he was taken as a hostage to Genoa, where he consummated his marriage with Helv
2 CONC is, whom he had wed when she was twelve. Most or all of their children were born in Genoa. Due to his captivity, he was not crowned until 1385. In Genoa he lived with his wife under hard circumstances for 9 years, and she gave bir
2 CONC th to their first son Janus in that city.
2 CONT
2 CONT =King=
2 CONT
2 CONT After Peter II's death in 1382, since Peter had no surviving issue, the Parliament of Cyprus decided James to be the king, while he was captive in Genoa. Genoese, in order to release him to go to Cyprus to become a king, they nego
2 CONC tiated with him and received his signature for agreement on February 2, 1383. Under that agreement, Genoese had new privileges for commercial activities. Famagusta was still under Genoese sovereignty, something that was never acce
2 CONC pted by either James and other kings after him and during his reign he tried to regain that city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Until he was released, the Kingdom of Cyprus was governed by 12 nobles. After he was released in 1383, he was not accepted, as it is referred by the historian Leontios Makhairas and returned to Genoa. Some nobles opposed the retur
2 CONC n of James, led by the brothers Perotte and Vilmonde de Montolivve, who were believing that with that situation they could become kings. James' opponents could not be beaten, until 1385. In April 1385, James came back again in Cyp
2 CONC rus and he went to Nicosia, where he was welcomed with great enthousiasm. He was crowned in May 1385 in Saint Sophia Cathedral. After his crowning, his opponents were arrested and punished.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was crowned King of Jerusalem in 1389 and in 1393, Leo VI of Armenia died, and James assumed the title of King of Armenia, and was formally given the title in 1396. That kingdom was by now reduced to the city of Korikos, which 
2 CONC had been in Cypriote hands since its conquest by Peter I of Cyprus. So when in 1382, Peter II died, James succeeded him, since Peter didn't have a son. He died in Nicosia.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Issue=
2 CONT
2 CONT He and his wife, Helvis of Brunswick-Grubenhagen had twelve children:
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Janus or John II of Lusignan (1375 – 1432), who succeeded him as king
2 CONT *  Philip of Lusignan (d. ca. 1430 or 1428/1432), Constable of Cyprus, unmarried, he had a natural son: Lancelot of Lusignan (d. after 1450), Cardinal, Latin Patriarch of Jerusalem
2 CONT * Henry of Lusignan (d. July 7, 1427), titular Prince of Galilee, a military leader in Egypt, killed in action at Khirokitia or Chirokhitia, married ca. 1406 his cousin Eleanor of Lusignan (d. ca. 1414), granddaughter of Jean de L
2 CONC usignan and second wife Alix of Ibelin, without issue, and had three bastard children
2 CONT *  Odo of Lusignan (d. 1421 in Palermo), Titular Seneschal of Jerusalem, in the service of the King of Aragon, (probably) married after March 19, 1406 his cousin Loysia de Lusignan, granddaughter of Jean de Lusignan and second wif
2 CONC e Alix d'Ibelin, without issue, without issue
2 CONT *  Hugh of Lusignan (d. August, 1442 in Geneva), Regent of Cyprus and Cardinal Archbishop of Nicosia
2 CONT *  Guy of Lusignan, Constable of Cyprus, unmarried and without issue
2 CONT *  an unknown daughter de Lusignan (d. 1374 in Rhodes), died young
2 CONT *  Jacqua of Lusignan (d. ca. 1397 or 1396/1398), unmarried and without issue
2 CONT *  Eschiva of Lusignan (d. after 1406), probably married to Count Sclavus von Asperg 
2 CONT *  Mary of Lusignan (1381 in Genoa – September 4, 1404 in Naples and buried there), married Ladislaus "le Magnanime", King of Naples and Jerusalem, Hungary and Dalmatia, etc. (July 14, 1376/February 11, 1377 in Naples – of poisoni
2 CONC ng August 6, 1414 at Naples and buried there) on February 12, 1403 in Naples, without issue
2 CONT *  Agnes of Lusignan (ca. 1382 – March 1, 1459 in Venasco), Abbess of Wunstorf
2 CONT *  Isabella of Lusignan, ca. 1415 married her cousin Peter of Lusignan, titular Count of Tripoli, regent of Cyprus and titular Constable and titular Seneschal of Jerusalem (d. February 10, 1451), grandson of John of Lusignan and s
2 CONC econd wife Alix of Ibelin, without issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his death, his son Janus succeeded to the throne.
