1 NOTE {geni:about_me} -https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_IV_of_Trebizond
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/TREBIZOND.htm#IoannesIVTrebizond<br/>
2 CONT 3.        ''' KALOIOANNES Megas Komnenos''' (before 1403-[Apr] 1460).  The Masarelli Vatican manuscript names (in order) Ioannes, Alexander, David, Maria and another unnamed daughter as the children of Alexios Komnenos Emperor in 
2 CONC Trebizond & his wife[156].  Despotes/co-Emperor 1417/26.  Laonicus Chalcocondylas records that "regi Ioanni" was exiled by his father after attempting to kill his mother "Cantacuzena" whom he suspected "had been intimate with the 
2 CONC protovestiarius" whom he killed[157].  He succeeded his father in 1429 as '''IOANNES IV Emperor in Trebizond'''.  He purchased immunity in 1453 by promising Sultan Mohammed II a handsome tribute[158].  Faced with the threat of inv
2 CONC asion from the Sultan, Ioannes allied himself with the Khan of the White Sheep, who became his son-in-law.  The post scriptum dated 5 May 1460, to a letter dated 19 Apr 1460 from the Genoese authorities in Caffa to the “Protectore
2 CONC s Sancti Georgii” of Genoa, notes “Imperator Trapezundarum diem suum obiit. In cuius imperio successit...dominus Dispotus eius frater”[159].  The precise time for news of the emperor’s death to arrive at Caffa (city in eastern Cri
2 CONC mea, now called Feodosia) from Trebizond is not known, nor therefore the precise date of his death.  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' firstly --- of Georgia, daughter of ALEKSANDRI I King of Georgia & his wife --- ([1411/12]-[before 1429]).  Laonicus Chalcocondylas records that "Ioannes" married "in Iberiam…regis Alexandri filiam"[160].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' secondly (before 1438) ---, daughter of [a Turk].  The Spanish traveller Pero Tafur records that Emperor Ioannes was "married to a daughter of a Turk" when he visited the Trebizond court in 1438[161].  According to Europäi
2 CONC sche Stammtafeln[162], she was the daughter of Devlet Berdi Khan of the Crimean Turks at Solkhat, but the basis for this is not known.  Emperor Ioannes IV & his [first] wife had one child:  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John IV Megas Komnenos (Greek: Ιωάννης Δ΄ Μέγας Κομνηνός, Iōannēs IV Megas Komnēnos), (c. 1403 – 1459) was Emperor of Trebizond from 1429 to 1459. He was a son of Emperor Alexios IV of Trebizond and Theodora Kantakouzene.
2 CONT
2 CONT John had been designated despotes by his father as early as 1417, but did not see eye-to-eye with his parents. In 1426 he murdered a courtier for allegedly having an affair with the Empress Theodora, and then proceeded to attack h
2 CONC is parents. His attempt on them was overcome by the palace staff, and John was forced to flee to Georgia.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Georgia John married Bagrationi, a daughter of King Alexander I but could not obtain sufficient support to establish himself in Trebizond. In 1427 he sailed off to the Genoese colony of Caffa in the Crimea. Here he managed to c
2 CONC harter a large galley and its crew, with which he returned to Trebizond in 1429. Although his father Alexios IV marched out against him, John managed to win the support of some of the nobility, who allowed John's agents to murder 
2 CONC his father while he was sleeping in his tent. John's brother Alexander, who had succeeded him as despotes proved no match for the invader and fled, and by late October 1429 John IV was recognized as emperor in Trebizond some time 
2 CONC before October 28, 1429.
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV began his reign by punishing the physical murderers of his father and burying him in state in the Metropolitan church. His reign was dominated by ongoing attempts to defend Trebizond from its Turkmen neighbors and the incr
2 CONC easingly aggressive Ottoman Empire to the west. The first challenge was posed by the ruler of Ardabil, Junayd, who defeated the emperor and briefly besieged Trebizond before giving up and raising the siege.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1442 the Ottoman Sultan Murad II sent out a fleet to plunder the shores and to attempt to capture the city. This expedition did not seriously impact Trebizond itself, but attacked Trebizond's dependencies in the Crimea and was 
2 CONC partly destroyed by a storm on its return journey. The Ottomans did not make another attack on the Empire of Trebizond until the reign of the next Sultan, Mehmed II.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1451 the Byzantine diplomat Sphrantzes arrived in Trebizond to arrange a marriage alliance between Emperor Constantine XI Palaiologos and a princess from either John IV's family or that of the kings of Georgia. This incident is
2 CONC  notable in Sphrantzes's Chronicle for the color it paints of the emperor, since Sphrantzes arrived in Trebizond having not heard yet of the death of the Ottoman sultan Murad II, which John gleefully related. He told Sphrantzes ho
2 CONC w great it was that now his empire could last longer and be blessed because of the youth of Mehmed II. Sphrantzes, however, was taken aback and explained to him that Mehmed's youth and seeming friendship to the new emperor Constan
2 CONC tine XI were only ploys, and that under John's brother-in-law John VIII the empire had been deeply in debt, but now his new emperor was trying to change that. The incident as described should well have served to John IV the new pe
2 CONC ril which had come forth. In 1453, after the Fall of Constantinople to Mehmed II, Trebizond and the Morea were left as the last remnants of the Byzantine imperial tradition. Mehmed II immediately summoned John to pay tribute in Co
2 CONC nstantinople and imposed heavy tolls on Trapezuntine and Venetian shipping through the straits. John apparently failed to cooperate, and in 1456 the Sultan dispatched his governor of Amasya to attack Trebizond by both land and sea
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The fall of his capital seeming imminent, John at long last made his submission and agreed to pay an annual tribute of 2,000 gold pieces. He sent his brother David to ratify the treaty before Mehmed II himself, which he did in 145
2 CONC 8, but the tribute was raised to 3,000 gold pieces. Aware of the Ottoman advance against the remaining Byzantine possessions in Greece, John attempted to bolster his position by resorting to marital alliances with neighboring prin
2 CONC ces. So he married off one of his daughters to Uzun Hasan of the Ak Koyunlu, and another daughter (or perhaps sister) to the Italian lord of Syros, Niccolò Crispo.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the increasingly desperate situation of Trebizond, John IV came to favor the idea of obtaining Western support by effecting a union with the Roman Catholic Church. As early as 1434 he responded to the letters of Pope Eugenius I
2 CONC V (in marked contrast to earlier emperors of Trebizond, who had ignored papal missives). In the late 1430s the Metropolitan of Trebizond joined the Byzantine clergy at the Council of Basel-Ferrara-Florence.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, these approaches to the Papacy were not marked by harmonious relations with the most important Western power concerned with Trebizond, the Republic of Genoa. Although John owed his throne to a Genoese crew, he repeatedly 
2 CONC failed to reimburse the Genoese for debts owed to them in 1431, and in 1441 refused to provide reparations for having ordered the seizure and pillage of a Genoese ship in 1435. Further diplomatic initiatives by Genoa failed in 144
2 CONC 3, and in 1447 the Genoese of Caffa advanced on Trebizond with their fleet, threatening to set up an embargo. The disputes were never fully settled and seriously injured commerce in the Black Sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's hostile attitude towards Genoa is credited to his fear of a potential Byzantino-Genoese alliance against him that could place his brother Alexander on the throne of Trebizond. Alexander had fled Trebizond for the Byzantine 
2 CONC court in 1429 and had eventually married Maria Gattilusio, the daughter of the Genoese lord of Lesbos. The apprehensiveness towards Genoa was contrasted by friendlier relations with Venice, although the Venetians never recovered t
2 CONC heir former influence in the Black Sea.
2 CONT
2 CONT John died some time before April 22, 1459, when his brother David is first attested as emperor of Trebizond.
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV was married twice, first to an unnamed daughter of King Alexander I of Georgia, and then to an unnamed Turkish princess. Probably by his first marriage he had a son and at least one, or possibly two daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alexios (1455–1463), beheaded at Constantinople
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodora, who married Uzun Hasan of Ak Koyunlu
2 CONT
2 CONT Eudokia (Valenza), who married Niccolò Crispo, lord of Syros; but she may have been a sister of John IV.
2 CONT
2 CONT John IV of Trebizond
2 CONT
2 CONT Komnenid dynasty
2 CONT
