1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_II_of_Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT John II
2 CONT King of Cyprus
2 CONT Reign	29 June 1432 – 28 July 1458
2 CONT Predecessor	Janus
2 CONT Successor	Charlotte
2 CONT Born	16 May 1418
2 CONT Died	28 July 1458 (aged 40)
2 CONT Spouse	Amadea Palaiologina of Montferrat
2 CONT Helena Palaiologina
2 CONT Issue	
2 CONT King James II (illeg.)
2 CONT Queen Charlotte
2 CONT Princess Cleopha
2 CONT House	Poitiers-Lusignan
2 CONT Father	Janus, King of Cyprus
2 CONT Mother	Charlotte of Bourbon
2 CONT John II or III of Cyprus (16 May 1418 – 28 July 1458) was the King of Cyprus and Armenia and also titular King of Jerusalem from 1432 to 1458. He was previously a titular Prince of Antioch.
2 CONT
2 CONT History
2 CONT Born 16 May 1418 in Nicosia, John was the son of king Janus of Cyprus and Charlotte of Bourbon.[1] In May, sometime between 1435 and 1440, he married Amadea Palaiologina of Monferrato, daughter of John Jacob Palaiologos, Marquess 
2 CONC of Montferrat. They had no children. His second wife, a distant relative of his first, was Helena Palaiologina,[2] only child and daughter of Theodore II Palaiologos, Despot of the Morea, and his wife Cleofa Malatesta
2 CONT
2 CONT By his second marriage he had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charlotte, Queen of Cyprus, married Prince John of Antioch.[3] She died childless
2 CONT Cleopha of Lusignan, died young
2 CONT John died in Nicosia on 28 July 1458 and his daughter Charlotte succeeded to the throne. During his rule, Corycus, the only Cypriot stronghold in mainland Anatolia, was lost to the Karamanids in 1448.
2 CONT
2 CONT John had an illegitimate son by Marietta de Patras[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT James II, King of Cyprus [4]
2 CONT John appointed James, Archbishop of Nicosia at the age of 16. James did not prove ideal archbishop material and was stripped of his title after murdering the royal chamberlain. His father eventually forgave him and restored him to
2 CONC  the Archbishopric. James and Helena were enemies, vying for influence over John. After Helena died in 1458, it appeared that John would appoint James as his successor, but John died before he could make it so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat-of-arms of Lusignan of Cyprus, Jerusalem and Lesser Armenia
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Murray 2006, p. 687.
2 CONT  Samson & Demetriou 2015, p. 9.
2 CONT  Boustronios 2005, p. 48.
2 CONT  Letts 2017, p. 252.
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT Boustronios, Georgios (2005). A Narrative of the Chronicle of Cyprus: 1456-1489. State University of New York Press.
2 CONT Letts, Malcolm, ed. (2017). The Pilgrimage of Arnold Von Harff, Knight, from Cologne: Through Italy, Syria, Egypt, Arabia, Ethiopia, Nubia, Palestine, Turkey, France and Spain, which He Accomplished in the Years 1496-1499. Taylor 
2 CONC & Francis.
2 CONT Murray, Alan V., ed. (2006). The Crusades: An Encyclopedia. Vol. 2. ABC-CLIO.
2 CONT Samson, Jim; Demetriou, Nicoletta, eds. (2015). Music in Cyprus. Ashgate Publishing Limited.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/2743-WW9
2 CONT
2 CONT https://dbpedia.org/page/John_II_of_Cyprus
