1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "In 1475 Henri Evelin came from Cyprus to Normandy, where he bought a small feif called Eveliniere in the " Balliage of Constantine,'' in other words, in the vice-county of Cotentin,whose capital was the ancient Co
2 CONC nstantia, now Coutances, in the department of La Manche. Coutances is a picturesque town with a beautiful Norman cathedral, situated half-way up a granite hill seven miles from the sea. From this Henri Evelin (whose descent from t
2 CONC he old Seigneur of Beirut is given in the Wotton MS. and who was twice married) descended various Seigneurs of Eveliniere, of Ramondiere of Valdesie and of Parie. The last name on the pedigree is that of William Evelin, physician 
2 CONC and councillor to King Henri IV, Louis XIII and Louis XIV, and who is said to be  "living in the present year, 1650."
