0 @N6000000002187617128@ NOTE Otanes
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Otanes (Old Persian Utâna): Persian nobleman, one of the seven conspirators who killed the Magian usurper Gaumâta and helped Darius I the Great become king (29 September 522 BC). Several years later, he added the Greek island Samo
1 CONC s to the Achaemenid empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT In March 522, a Magian named Gaumâta seized power in the Achaemenid empire, claiming to be the brother of the legitimate king Cambyses, Smerdis. Gaumâta could do this, because Smerdis had been killed secretly. Immediately, Cambyse
1 CONC s advanced to the usurper, but he died before he reached Persia; the false Smerdis was able to rule for several months.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Greek researcher Herodotus, Otanes, the brother of the mother of Cambyses and the real Smerdis, was the first to become suspicious of the false Smerdis. From his daughter Phaedymia, who was married to the king, he
1 CONC  learned that Smerdis was in reality a Magian. On hearing this news, Otanes invited Aspathines and Gobryas to discuss the usurpation. Together, they decided to invite three other conspirators: Hydarnes, Intaphrenes and Megabyzus. 
1 CONC They were still making plans, when Darius arrived and sided with them. He convinced the seven to strike immediately and not to wait, as Otanes had proposed. On 29 September 522 BCE, the seven killed the false Smerdis.
1 CONT
1 CONT That Otanes was involved in the killing of Gaumâta is confirmed by another source, the Behistun inscription. This inscription also tells us the name of Otanes' father, which was Thukhra. Herodotus is mistaken when he calls him Pha
1 CONC rnaspes, but it is possible to rescue him by accepting the hypothesis that Thukra was a nickname ('redhead').
1 CONT
1 CONT Herodotus tells us that after the murder, the seven men discussed the future constitution of Persia. Otanes said that Persia ought to be a democracy; Megabyzus argued for an oligarchy and Darius said that monarchy was the best kin
1 CONC d of rule. The other four noblemen sided with him, and Darius became king. Herodotus stresses that this discussion really took place. Probably, he has misunderstood a debate about the future of Persia: was it to be a centralized m
1 CONC onarchy (which it became) or was it to be a loosely organized federation (as it had been)?
1 CONT
1 CONT When it was decided that Persia was to be a monarchy and Darius was to be its king, Otanes choose to stay aloof of it and receive special rights:
1 CONT
1 CONT To this day, the family of Otanes continues to be the only free family in Persia, and submits to the king only so far as the members of it may choose. They are bound, however, to observe the law like anyone else.
1 CONT
1 CONT    Herodotus, Histories 3.83
1 CONT That is, at least, Herodotus' story. But there may be more than meets the eye: perhaps Otanes was a rival candidate to the Persian throne. There are many elements in Herodotus' story that point into this direction. In the first pl
1 CONC ace, we see that Otanes started the conspiracy and that Darius sided with them later. In the second place, Otanes and Darius argued for opposite ideas on two occasions: should the seven wait or strike immediately and should Persia
1 CONC  be a democracy or a monarchy? In the third place, Otanes had a powerful claim to the throne because he was the brother of Cassandane, the queen of king Cyrus the Great, and the father of Phaedymia, the queen of Cambyses and the f
1 CONC alse Smerdis. (Darius' claim to the throne was based on the fact that he belonged to a younger branch of the family of Cyrus and Cambyses, the Achaemenids.)
1 CONT
1 CONT It is likely that Otanes, who, according to Herodotus, decided to stay aloof from Persian politics, prepared the road for Darius to become king. It was necessary: after the assassination of Gaumâta, the Babylonians had revolted an
1 CONC d the crisis was acute. Otanes stepped aside and Darius honored him by marrying his daughter Phaedymia, who had already been married to Cambyses and the false Smerdis.When Darius married her, his rule became more legitimate. At th
1 CONC e same time, Otanes married a sister of Darius. They probably were the parents of Amestris, who was to marry king Xerxes.
1 CONT
1 CONT That the king trusted Otanes, is also suggested by the fact that he ordered his father-in-law, who was probably serving as satrap of Lydia, to conquer the Greek island Samos (c.517 BC). This island had been without strong leader s
1 CONC ince an earlier Lydian satrap, Oroetus, had executed Polycrates of Samos. Its new, pro-Persian ruler, was to be a man named Syloson. Herodotus tells us:
1 CONT
1 CONT As for Samos, the Persians took the entire population like fish in a drag-net, and presented Syloson with an empty island. Some years later, however, Otanes contracted some sort of disease of the genital organs and that, in conjun
1 CONC ction with a dream he had, induced him to repopulate the place.
1 CONT
1 CONT    Histories 3.149;
1 CONT    tr. Aubrey de Selincourt
1 CONT This is the last piece of information about Otanes. In 513 BC, a new satrap was appointed in Lydia, Artaphernes. Probably, Otanes had died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Otanes had a son Patiramphes, who served as the driver of the chariot of King Xerxes during his campaign to Greece. As we have already seen above, Otanes was probably also the father of Xerxes' first wife, queen Amestris.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Reference
1 CONT This entry incorporates public domain text originally by Jona Lendering ©. 
0 @N6000000002187600896@ NOTE Artaxerxes I of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes I was king of the Persian Empire from 464 BC to 424 BC. He belonged to the Achaemenid dynasty and was the successor of Xerxes I. He is mentioned in two books of the Bible, Ezra and Nehemiah. He allowed the Jews to rebui
1 CONC ld Jerusalem. He was followed on the throne by his son Xerxes II.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name as given is the Greek form; the Persian form is Artakhshathra. He was later called Ardeshir.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Persia had been defeated at Eurymedon, military action between Greece and Persia had come to a standstill. When Artaxerxes I took power, he began a new tradition of drawing off the Athenians by funding their enemies in Greec
1 CONC e. This indirectly caused the Athenians to move the treasury of the Delian League from the island of Delos to the Athenian acropolis. This funding practice inevitably prompted renewed fighting in 450 BC, where the Greeks attacked 
1 CONC at the Battle of Cyprus. After Cimon's failure to attain much in this expedition, "The Peace of Callias" was agreed between Athens, Argos and Persia in 449.
0 @N6000000002187599978@ NOTE Haman (Bible)
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT  The Punishment of Haman, by Michaelangelo.Haman (or Haman the Agagite ??? ?????) (possibly Persian "magnificent") was a 6th Century BCE Persian noble and vizier of the empire under Persian King Ahasuerus, generally identified as 
1 CONC Xerxes I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Haman in the Hebrew Bible 
1 CONT 2 Haman in other sources 
1 CONT 2.1 Midrash 
1 CONT 2.2 Josephus 
1 CONT 2.3 Qur'an 
1 CONT 3 Purim traditions 
1 CONT 4 Modern usage 
1 CONT 5 References 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Haman in the Hebrew Bible
1 CONT Haman is the villain in the Book of Esther. He is described as the son of Hammedatha the Agagite, presumably indicating his descent from Agag, king of Amalek.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the story, Haman and his wife Zeresh instigate a plot to kill all the Jews of ancient Persia. Haman attempts to convince Ahasuerus to order the killing of Mordechai and all the Jews of the lands he ruled. The plot is foiled by 
1 CONC Queen Esther, the king's recent wife, who is herself a Jew. Haman and his 10 sons are hanged from the gallows that had originally been built to hang Mordechai.
1 CONT
1 CONT After these things king Ahasuerus promoted Haman the son of Hammedatha the Agagite, and advanced him, and set his seat above all the princes who were with him. And all the king's servants, who were in the king's gate, bowed, and d
1 CONC id obeisance to Haman; for the king had so commanded concerning him. But Mordechai did not bow, or do him obeisance. (Esther, 3:1-2) 
1 CONT And When Haman saw that Mordechai did not bow or do him obedience, then Haman was full of wrath. But he disdained to lay hands on Mordechai alone; for they had told him of the people of Mordechai; so that Haman sought to destroy a
1 CONC ll the Jews who were throughout the whole kingdom of Ahasuerus, the people of Mordechai. (Esther, 3:5-6) 
1 CONT Later, Queen Esther learns that her people are in danger so she risks her own life on behalf of the salvation of the Hebrews living in Ancient Persia.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Xerxes asked Queen Esther, "Who is he? Where is the man who has dared to do such a thing?" 6 Esther said, "The adversary and enemy is this vile Haman." Then Haman was terrified before the king and queen. 7 The king got up in 
1 CONC a rage, left his wine and went out into the palace garden. But Haman, realizing that the king had already decided his fate, stayed behind to beg Queen Esther for his life. 8 Just as the king returned from the palace garden to the 
1 CONC banquet hall, Haman was falling on the couch where Esther was reclining. The king exclaimed, "Will he even molest the queen while she is with me in the house?" 9 As soon as the word left the king's mouth, they covered Haman's face
1 CONC . 9 Then Harbona, one of the eunuchs attending the king, said, "A gallows seventy-five feet [b] high stands by Haman's house. He had it made for Mordecai, who spoke up to help the king." 10 The king said, "Hang him on it!" 10 So t
1 CONC hey hanged Haman on the gallows he had prepared for Mordecai. Then the king's fury subsided. (Esther, 7:6-10)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Haman in other sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Midrash
1 CONT In Rabbinical tradition, Haman is considered an archetype of evil and persecutor of the Jews. Having attempted to exterminate the Jews of Persia, and rendering himself thereby their worst enemy, Haman naturally became the center o
1 CONC f many Talmudic legends. Being at one time in extreme want, he sold himself as a slave to Mordechai (Meg. 15a). He was a barber at Kefar Karzum for the space of twenty-two years (ib. 16a). Haman had an idolatrous image embroidered
1 CONC  on his garments, so that those who bowed to him at command of the king bowed also to the image (Esth. R. vii.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Haman was also an astrologer, and when he was about to fix the time for the massacre of the Jews he first cast lots to ascertain which was the most auspicious day of the week for that purpose. Each day, however, proved to be under
1 CONC  some influence favorable to the Jews. He then sought to fix the month, but found that the same was true of each month; thus, Nisan was favorable to the Jews because of the Passover sacrifice; Iyyar, because of the small Passover.
1 CONC  But when he arrived at Adar he found that its zodiacal sign was Pisces, and he said, "Now I shall be able to swallow them as fish which swallow one another" (Esth. R. vii.; Targ. Sheni iii.).
1 CONT
1 CONT Haman had 365 counselors, but the advice of none was so good as that of his wife, Zeresh. She induced Haman to build a gallows for Mordechai, assuring him that this was the only way in which he would be able to prevail over his en
1 CONC emy, for hitherto the just had always been rescued from every other kind of death. As God foresaw that Haman himself would be hanged on the gallows He asked which tree would volunteer to serve as the instrument of death. Each tree
1 CONC , declaring that it was used for some holy purpose, objected to being soiled by the unclean body of Haman. Only the thorn-tree could find no excuse, and therefore offered itself for a gallows (Esth. R. ix.; Midr. Abba Gorion vii.,
1 CONC  ed. Buber, Wilna, 1886; in Targum Sheni this is narrated somewhat differently).
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Josephus
1 CONT Haman is mentioned by Josephus in his Antiquities of the Jews. Josephus' account of the story is drawn from the Septuagint translation of the Book of Esther and from other Greek and Jewish sources, some no longer extant.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Qur'an
1 CONT Template:Haman (Islam) Haman is also mentioned six times in the Qur'an, as an intimate of Pharaoh in the time of Moses. While they are obviously different, elements of the Qur'an's version of Haman may come from the Persian Haman 
1 CONC including his name and position as a royal builder.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Purim traditions
1 CONT Main article: Purim
1 CONT The Jewish holiday of Purim commemorates the story of the deliverance of the Jews and the defeat of Haman. On that day, the Book of Esther is read publicly and much noise and tumult is raised at every mention of his name. A specia
1 CONC l noisemaker called a Grogger is used to express disdain for Haman. Pastry known as Hamantaschen are traditionally eaten on this day.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Modern usage
1 CONT To "hang higher than Haman" is a phrase that has come to mean someone who has committed an act so egregious as to be destined for a very terrible punishment.
1 CONT
1 CONT Haman is also mentioned in South Park episode #309, Jewbilee.
1 CONT
1 CONT A major antagonist of Zeta Gundam, and the main antagonist in its sequel series, Gundam ZZ, is Haman Karn, a Zeon regent who usurps the power of the rightful ruler.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1901–1906 Jewish Encyclopedia, a publication now in the public domain. 
0 @N6000000002187617206@ NOTE Xerxes II of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Xerxes II was a Persian king and the son and successor of Artaxerxes I. After a reign of forty-five days, he was assassinated in 424 BC by his brother Sogdianus, who in turn was murdered by Darius II. He is an obscure historical f
1 CONC igure known primarily from the writings of Ctesias. He was reportedly the only legitimate son of Artaxerxes I and his Queen Damaspia. He is known to have served as Crown Prince.
1 CONT
1 CONT The last inscription mentioning Artaxerxes I being alive can be dated to December 24, 424 BC. Xerxes apparently succeeded to the throne but two of his illegitimate brothers claimed it for themselves. The first was Sogdianus, son b
1 CONC y concubine Alogyne of Babylon. The second was Darius II, son by concubine Cosmartidene of Babylon. He was married to their common half-sister Parysatis, daughter of Artaxerxes I and his concubine Andia of Babylon.
1 CONT
1 CONT Xerxes was apparently only recognized in Persia and Sogdianus in Elam. Ochus' first inscription as Darius II can be dated to January 10, 423 BC. He was already satrap of Hyrcania and was soon recognized by Media, Babylonia and Egy
1 CONC pt. Xerxes II only ruled forty five days. He was reportedly murdered while drunk by Pharnacyas, and Menostanes on Sogdianus' orders. Sogdianus apparently gained the support of his regions. Sogdianus was killed a few months later. 
1 CONC Darius II became the sole ruler of the Persian Empire and would reign till 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenid dynasty
1 CONT Born:  ??; Died: 424 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes I King (Shah) of Persia
1 CONT 424 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sogdianus 
1 CONT Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 424 
0 @N6000000002187600795@ NOTE Parysatis
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Parysatis was the illegitimate daughter of Artaxerxes I, Emperor of Persia and Andia of Babylon. She was the half-sister of Xerxes II, Sogdianus and Darius II. She married her half-brother Darius and had two sons, Artaxerxes III a
1 CONC nd Cyrus the Younger. When her husband died, she supported her younger son Cyrus. When Cyrus was defeated in the Battle of Cunaxa she blamed the starpt Tissapharnes for the death of her son. She later had Tissapharnes assassainate
1 CONC d.
0 @N6000000002187617187@ NOTE Sogdianus of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sogdianus, king of Persia (424 - 423 BC). He is an obscure historical figure known primarily from the writings of Ctesias. He was reportedly an illegitimate son of Artaxerxes I by his concubine Alogyne of Babylon.
1 CONT
1 CONT The last inscription mentioning Artaxerxes I being alive can be dated to December 24, 424 BC. His death resulted in at least three of his sons proclaiming themselves Kings. The first was Xerxes II, who was reportedly his only legi
1 CONC timate son by Queen Damaspia and was formerly Crown Prince. He was apparently only recognized in Persia. The second was Sogdianus himself, possibly recognized in Elam. The third was Ochus, son of Artaxerxes I by his concubine Cosm
1 CONC artidene of Babylon and satrap of Hyrcania. Ochus was also married to their common half-sister Parysatis, daughter of Artaxerxes I and his concubine Andia of Babylon. The first inscription of Ochus as Darius II can be dated to Jan
1 CONC uary 10, 423 BC. He seems to have been recognized by Media, Babylonia and Egypt.
1 CONT
1 CONT This chaotic state of affairs would prove short-lived. Xerxes II only ruled for forty-five days. He was reportedly murdered while drunk by Pharnacyas and Menostanes on Sogdianus' orders. Sogdianus apparently gained the support of 
1 CONC his regions. He was himself killed by Arbarios, commander of the cavalry. He had only reigned for six months and fifteen days. Darius II became the sole ruler of the Persian Empire and would reign till 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Achaemenid dynasty
1 CONT Born:  ??; Died: 424 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Xerxes II King (Shah) of Persia
1 CONT 424–423 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Darius II 
1 CONT Pharaoh of Egypt
1 CONT 424–423 
0 @N6000000002187600784@ NOTE Darius II of Persia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Darius II, originally called Ochus and often surnamed Nothus (from Greek ?????, meaning 'bastard'), was emperor of Persia from 423 BC to 404 BC.
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes I, who died shortly after December 24, 424 BC, was followed by his son Xerxes II. After a month and a half Xerxes was murdered by his brother Secydianus or Sogdianus (the form of the name is uncertain). His illegitimate
1 CONC  brother, Ochus, satrap of Hyrcania, rebelled against Sogdianus, and after a short fight killed him, and suppressed by treachery the attempt of his own brother Arsites to imitate his example. Ochus adopted the name Darius (in the 
1 CONC chronicles he is called Nothos, meaning "the bastard"). Neither Xerxes II nor Secydianus occurs in the dates of the numerous Babylonian tablets from Nippur; here the reign of Darius II follows immediately after that of Artaxerxes 
1 CONC I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Of Darius's reign we know very little (a rebellion of the Medes in 409 BC is mentioned in Xenophon), except that he was quite dependent on his wife Parysatis. In the excerpts from Ctesias some harem intrigues are recorded, in whic
1 CONC h he played a disreputable part. As long as the power of Athens remained intact he did not meddle in Greek affairs; even the support which the Athenians in 413 BC gave to the rebel Amorges in Caria would not have roused him, had n
1 CONC ot the Athenian power been broken in the same year before Syracuse. He gave orders to his satraps in Asia Minor, Tissaphernes and Pharnabazus, to send in the overdue tribute of the Greek towns, and to begin a war with Athens; for 
1 CONC this purpose they entered into an alliance with Sparta. In 408 BC he sent his son Cyrus to Asia Minor, to carry on the war with greater energy. In 404 BC Darius II died after a reign of nineteen years, and was followed by Artaxerx
1 CONC es II.
1 CONT
1 CONT Based on an article from a 1911 encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sogdianus Great King of Media and Persia
1 CONT 423–404 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes II 
1 CONT King of Egypt
1 CONT 423–404 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Amyrtaeus 
0 @N6000000002187600729@ NOTE Artaxerxes II of Persia
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes II Memnon (c. 436 - 358 BC) was king of Persia from 404 BC until his death. He defended his position against his brother Cyrus the Younger, who was defeated and killed at the Battle of Cunaxa in 401 BC, and against a re
1 CONC volt of the provincial governors, the satraps (366 - 358). He also became involved in a war with Persia's erstwhile allies, the Spartans, who, under Agesilaus, invaded Asia Minor. To keep the Spartans busy, Artaxerxes subsidized t
1 CONC heir enemies in Greece - the Athenians, Thebans, and Corinthians, especially - to keep them busy back at home, in what would become known as the Corinthian War. In 386 BC Artaxerxes II stabbed his allies in the back and came to an
1 CONC  arrangement with Sparta, and in the Treaty of Antalcidas forced his erstwhile allies to come to terms. This treaty restored control of the Greek cities of Ionia and Aeolis on the Anatolian coast to the Persians, while giving Spar
1 CONC ta dominance on the Greek mainland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although thus rather successful against the Greeks, Artaxerxes had more trouble with the Egyptians, who had successfully revolted against him at the beginning of his reign. An attempt to reconquer Egypt in 373 BC was completely un
1 CONC successful, but in his waning years the Persians did manage to defeat a joint Egyptian-Spartan effort tto conquer Phoenicia.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Building Projects
1 CONT Much of Artaxerxes's money was spent on building projects – he restored the palace of Darius I at Susa, and also the fortifications – including a strong redoubt at the southeast corner of the enclosure and gave Ecbatana a new apad
1 CONC ana and sculptures. He seems not to have built much at Persepolis.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT See also:
1 CONT
1 CONT Artaxerxes I 
1 CONT History of Persia 
1 CONT The Anabasis 
1 CONT Ten Thousand (Greek) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Darius II Great King of Media and Persia
1 CONT 404–358 BC Succeeded by:
1 CONT Artaxerxes III 
