0 @N6000000002187597467@ NOTE Pacorus II of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Pacorus II of Parthia ruled the Parthian Empire from about 78 to 105. A son of Vonones II and brother of Vologases I, he was given the kingdom of Media Atropatene by the latter after his succession to the throne. After Vologases' 
1 CONC death, Pacorus revolted against his brother's son and successor, Vologases II (c. 78–80), defeating and deposing him.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Dio Cassius Pacorus sold the kingdom of Osroene to Abgar VII, and according to Ammianus Marcellinus he enlarged the Parthian Ctesiphon and built its walls.
1 CONT
1 CONT On his numerous coins he always calls himself "Arsaces Pacorus". This mention of his proper name, together with the royal name Arsaces, shows that his kingdom was disputed by rivals. Three of them we know from coins; in addition t
1 CONC o Vologases II, they included Artabanus III, c. 80–90 and Vologases III, from about 105.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pacorus died about 105; he was succeeded by his brother Osroes I.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vologases II King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Osroes I and Vologases III 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the Encyclopædia Britannica Eleventh Edition, a publication now in the public domain. 
1 CONT Dio Cassius, lxviii, 17. 
1 CONT Ammianus Marcellinus, Res Gestae, xxiii, 6, 23. 
0 @N6000000001354417867@ NOTE Event: Ruled 51 - 77, King of Parthia 1 3
1 CONT Note:
1 CONT Vologeses I (d. c. AD 80), king of Parthia (reigned c. AD 51-80), theson of the previous king, Vonones II, by a Greek concubine.
1 CONT Vologeses gave the kingdom of Media Atropatene to his brother Pacorusand occupied Armenia for another brother, Tiridates. Parthian controlof Armenia, however, led to a long war with the Romans (AD 54-63). A peace was finally concl
1 CONC uded by which Tiridates was acknowledged as a Roman client king in Armenia. The power of Vologeses was further weakened by an attack by the nomadic Dahae and Shakas, a rebellion of the Hyrcanians, an invasion by Alani tribesmen in
1 CONC  Media and Armenia, and theusurpation of his son Vardanes II. Vologeses' reign was also marked by a decided reaction against Hellenism; he built Vologesias near Ctesiphon with the intention of drawing to the new town the inhabitan
1 CONC ts and trade of the Greek city Seleucia on the Tigris. [Encyclopaedia Britannica CD '97, VOLOGESES I]
1 CONT Note: After the reign of Vologaesus I, the utmost confusion prevailedin Parthia, with two or more kings (all of them little known) ruling at the same time and certainly challenged by other claimants.
0 @N6000000002187597446@ NOTE Vologases I of Parthia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vologases I of Parthia ruled the Parthian Empire (a forerunner of today's Iran) from about 51 to 78. Son of Vonones II by a Greek concubine, he succeeded his father in 51. He gave the kingdom of Media Atropatene to his brother Pac
1 CONC orus II, and occupied Armenia for another brother, Tiridates. This led to a long war with the Roman Empire (54–63), which was ably conducted by the Roman general Corbulo.
1 CONT
1 CONT The power of Vologases was weakened by an attack of the Dahan and Sacan nomads, a rebellion of the Hyrcanians, and the usurpation of his son Vardanes II. According to Josephus, he was prevented from attacking the vassal king of Ad
1 CONC iabene by an invasion of the eastern nomads. At last a peace was concluded, by which Tiridates was acknowledged as king of Armenia, but had to become a vassal of the Romans; he went to Rome, where Roman emperor Nero gave him back 
1 CONC the diadem; from that time an Arsacid dynasty ruled in Armenia under Roman supremacy.
1 CONT
1 CONT Vologases was satisfied with this result, and honored the memory of Nero (Suetonius Nero, 57), though he stood in good relations with Vespasian also, to whom he offered an army of 40,000 archers in the war against Vitellius. Soon 
1 CONC afterwards the Alani, a great nomadic tribe beyond the Caucasus, invaded Media and Armenia; Vologases applied in vain for help to Vespasian. It appears that the Persian losses in the east also could not be repaired; Hyrcania remai
1 CONC ned an independent kingdom. Vologases died in about 78 and was succeeded by his son Vologases II.
1 CONT
1 CONT His reign is marked by a decided reaction against Hellenism; he built Vologesocerta (Balashkert) in the neighborhood of Ctesiphon with the intention of drawing to this new town the inhabitants of Seleucia on the Tigris. Another to
1 CONC wn founded by him is Vologesias on a canal of the Euphrates, south of Babylon (near Hira). On some of his coins the initials of his name appear in Aramaic letters.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Vonones II King of Parthia Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vologases II 
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT References
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the 1911 Encyclopædia Britannica, which is in the public domain. 
1 CONT Tacitus, Annals xii–xv; Histories, iv. 
1 CONT Josephus, Antiquities of the Jews, vii, xx; History of the Jewish War, vii. 
1 CONT Dio Cassius lxii, lxiii, lxvi. 
1 CONT Suetonius, Vespasian, 6; Nero, 57; Domitian, 2. 
1 CONT Aurelius Victor Epit. 15, 4. 
1 CONT Noldeke, Zeitschnjt der deutschen-morgenl. Gesellschaft, xxviii. 
1 CONT Pliny vi. 122. 
