0 @N6000000002187597042@ NOTE Isaac of Armenia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT Isaac of Armenia, or Sahak (338-439) was Catholicos (or Patriarch) of Armenia. He is sometimes known as "Isaac the Great," and as "Parthev" owing to his Parthian origin.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was son of the Christian Saint Narses and descended from the family of Saint Gregory the Illuminator. Left an orphan at a very early age, he received in Constantinople an excellent literary education, particularly in the Easter
1 CONC n languages. After his election as patriarch he devoted himself to the religious and scientific training of his people. Armenia was then passing through a grave crisis. In 387 it had lost its independence and been divided between 
1 CONC the Byzantine Empire and Persia; each division had at its head an Armenian but feudatory king. In the Byzantine territory, however, the Armenians were forbidden the use of the Syriac language, until then exclusively used in divine
1 CONC  worship: for this the Greek language was to be substituted, and the country gradually hellenized; in the Persian districts, on the contrary, Greek was absolutely prohibited, while Syriac was greatly favoured. In this way the anci
1 CONC ent culture of the Armenians was in danger of disappearing and national unity was seriously compromised.
1 CONT
1 CONT To save both Isaac invented, with the aid of Saint Mesrob, the Armenian alphabet and began to translate the Christian Bible; their translation from the Syriac Peshito was revised by means of the Septuagint, and even, it seems, fro
1 CONC m the Hebrew text (between 410 and 430). The liturgy also, hitherto Syrian was translated into Armenian, drawing at the same time on the liturgy of Saint Basil of Caesarea, so as to obtain for the new service a national colour. Is
1 CONC aac had already established schools for higher education with the aid of disciples whom he had sent to study at Edessa, Melitene, Constantinople, and elsewhere. Through them he now had the principal masterpieces of Greek and Syria
1 CONC n Christian literature translated, e.g. the writings of Athanasius, Cyril of Jerusalem, Basil, the two Gregorys (Gregory of Nazianzus and Gregory of Nyssa), John Chrysostom, Ephrem the Syrian, etc. Armenian literature in its golde
1 CONC n age was, therefore, mainly a borrowed literature.
1 CONT
1 CONT Through Isaac's efforts the churches and monasteries destroyed by the Persians were rebuilt, education was cared for in a generous way, Zoroastrianism which Shah Yazdegerd I tried to set up was cast out, and three councils held to
1 CONC  re-establish ecclesiastical discipline. Isaac is said to have been the author of liturgical hymns. Two letters, written by him to Theodosius II and to Atticus of Constantinople, have been preserved. A third letter addressed to Sa
1 CONC int Proclus of Constantinople was not written by him, but dates from the tenth century. Neither did he have any share, as was wrongly ascribed to him, in the Council of Ephesus of 431, though, in consequence of disputes which aros
1 CONC e in Armenia between the followers of Nestorius and the disciples of Acacius of Melitene and Rabbulas, Isaac and his church did appeal to Constantinople and through Saint Proclus obtained the desired explanations. A man of enlight
1 CONC ened piety and of very austere life, Isaac owed his deposition by the king in 426 to his great independence of character. In 430 he was allowed to resume his patriarchal throne. In his extreme old age he seems to have withdrawn in
1 CONC to solitude, dying at the age of 110. Neither the exact year nor the precise month of his death is known, but it seems to have occurred between 439 and 441. Several days are consecrated to his memory in the Armenian Church.
1 CONT
1 CONT This article incorporates text from the public domain Catholic Encyclopedia.
