0 @N6000000005024494595@ NOTE Catholic
1 CONT 7. Jean de5 BETHENCOURT (II) (Jean4, Regnault3, Philippe2, Jean1);52,53 born circa 1320 in Sigy-en-Bray, Argueil, Dieppe, Seine-Maritime, France;54 married Isabeau de St. Martin, daughter of Jean de St. Martin and Isabeau de Harco
1 CONC urt, 1338 in France;55,56,57 died 1357 in the Battle of Honfleur fighting against the troops of the Black Prince.58,59,60
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT He is referred to as Jean I in The Canarian which incorrectly indicates his father as Regnault de Bethencourt, but then includes information from 1346-1357, including his marriage to Isabeau de St. Martin.61 He was also known as J
1 CONC ehan de Béthencourt (II) suffix added for clarification.62 After 1337 Jean de Bethencourt (II) was Lord of Béthencourt, of Saint Vincent de Rouvray, and of Grainville.63,64,65 Like his father, he followed a military career. He was
1 CONC  registered in the regiment of the host of René de Belleval, in the division of the Count of Alençon, for the war against England. He also served with his company of 10 men in the region of Poitou.66 He participated in the siege o
1 CONC f Tournai, Flanders, in 1340.67 He was took part in the battles of Ploërmel and of Honfleur under the command of the marshal of Normandy, Robert de Clermont in 1357.68
1 CONT
1 CONT Isabeau de ST. MARTIN69 was also known as Anne Isabeau de Clermont.70 She was also known as Anne Isabelle de Clermont.71 She was born after 1320 in Saint-Martin-le-Gaillard, Eu, Dieppe, Seine-Maritime, France; .72 (Monsieur Pierre
1 CONC  Cleret has presented a different genealogy for this line. He states that the wife of Jehan (Jean) de St. Martin, Isabeau's father, was not Isabeau de Harcourt, but Isabelle Martel de Basqueville. Her ancestor was Guillaume de Bas
1 CONC queville who was married to the great-granddaughter of Herfaut, brother of Duchess Gonor, wife of Richard Sans Peur, 3d Duke of Normandy. Guillaume de Basqueville was the son of Nicolas de Basqueville, whose father, Baldric le Teu
1 CONC ton, married a niece of Gilbert de Brionne, who was the grandson of Richard Sans Peur, son of Guillaume Longue Épée, son of Rollon, 1st Duke of Normandy. Nicolas married Albreda, great-granddaughter of Harfaut, sister of Guillaume
1 CONC  Fitzaubert).73,74,75 After 1353 Isabeau sold or pretended to sell her inherited castle at Saint-Martin to Mathieu de Braquemont.76 She married Mathieu de Braquemont, son of Regnault de Braquemont (I), after 1357 in France.77 Befo
1 CONC re 1360 in the division of her father's property, the castle must have fallen to Isabeau and, in this way, by the said sale or by her second marriage, passed to the possession of Mathieu de Braquemont. In it then lived Mathieu wit
1 CONC h his wife and probably their children. It followed, however, that meanwhile, war broke out between the King of France and Charles the Bad, King of Navarre, the Braquemonts taking the side of the latter. For this reason, the King 
1 CONC of France ordered that the castle belonging to Mathieu be seized, and Pierre d'Auxy was charged with the task. Pierre was the son of Hugues d'Auxy, Lord of Dompierre, and his wife Isabeau de Marigny who was descended from the anci
1 CONC ent barons of Saint-Martin; he was, therefore, cousin once removed of Isabeau de Saint-Martin. She was still young and attractive. Pierre fell in love with her and sequestered her, perhaps with her consent. A bastard appears to ha
1 CONC ve been born from this adulterous affair, taking the name of Dompierre and is presumed to be the nobleman of this name who accompanied Jean (IV) in the expedition to the Canary Islands. Enraged by the seizure and by the sequestrat
1 CONC ion and adultery of his wife, Mathieu killed Pierre, later begging pardon for this crime from the King.78 In 1376 Isabeau put in motion proceedings against various people, including her sisters, proceedings in which she complains 
1 CONC of having been wronged in the division of her father's property.79
0 @N6000000002322337060@ NOTE At age 3 he became the 1st Russian ruler to be formally crowned. The 1st 13
1 CONT years of his official reign marked great periods of internal reform, external
1 CONT expansion and centralization of state power, but, after 1560, he displayed
1 CONT erratic behavior and wanton brutality, such as ordering the slaying of 1,000s
1 CONT in the town of Novgorod because of rumors of conspiracy against him in 1570.
1 CONT In 1581, in a fit of anger, he struck and killed his favorite son. He resorted
1 CONT to torture, exile, and execution to suppress those plotting against him.
